+++ b/docs/memory/decision-log/dl-002-json-patch-rfc-6902-semantics-for-applypatch.md
@@ -0,0 +1,55 @@
+---
+id: dl-002-json-patch-rfc-6902-semantics-for-applypatch
+type: decision-log
+title: "JSON Patch (RFC 6902) semantics for applyPatch"
+status: approved
+---
+
+## Context
+
+RFC 6902 leaves several behaviours to the implementer, and `applyPatch` is a second building block
+other json-toolkit features and callers will depend on, so these choices need to be fixed now rather
+than redecided per caller. `applyPatch` is built on `resolvePointer`'s existing JSON Pointer rules
+(dl-001) for navigating to an operation's target.
+
+## Decision
+
+- **Root pointer (`""`) as a target**: `add` and `replace` at the root pointer replace the whole
+  document with the operation's value, treating the document itself as if it were a container member —
+  the same uniform rule intermediate-token navigation already gives every other path. `remove` and
+  `move`'s `from` reject the root pointer instead: removing "the document" has no valid JSON value to
+  leave behind, unlike deleting a member of an object or array. `copy`'s `from` accepts the root pointer
+  freely, since reading the whole document requires no removal.
+- **`move` cannot move a value into its own descendant**: per RFC 6902 §4.4, `from` must not be a
+  proper prefix of `path`; `applyPatch` rejects such operations before touching the document, rather
+  than producing a cyclic or data-losing result from a naive remove-then-add.
+- **Array index `-` means "append", and only for insertion**: `add`'s destination (and the destination
+  `move`/`copy` write to) accepts `-` or any index from `0` to the array's current length inclusive, to
+  insert before that position or append. `remove`, `replace`, and any `from`/`test` target keep
+  `resolvePointer`'s stricter rule — the index must already exist (`0` to `length - 1`, never `-`) —
+  since those operations reference a value that must already be there.
+- **`copy`, `move`, `add`, and `replace` never let the result alias the input**: the value placed in
+  the document is deep-cloned (`structuredClone`) before insertion, whether it comes from the
+  operation's own `value` field or from another location in the document (`copy`/`move`). This keeps
+  the returned document free of shared mutable references with both the original `document` and the
+  `patch` array, satisfying "must not mutate its input" for every part of the input, not just the
+  document's own pre-existing structure.
+- **`test` uses structural equality, not reference equality**: two values are equal when they are
+  primitively identical, or are arrays of equal length with pairwise-equal elements in the same order,
+  or are objects with the same set of own keys and pairwise-equal values — key order does not matter,
+  array order does. This matches "the same value" in RFC 6902 §4.6 for JSON values, which have no
+  identity of their own.
+- **Operation validation happens per-operation, not as an up-front pass**: each array entry is checked
+  (plain object, a recognised `op`, a string `path`, and the `value`/`from` that operation requires)
+  immediately before it is applied. A later operation's invalid shape or failed precondition still
+  leaves the original `document` untouched, because every operation up to that point was applied to a
+  private deep clone that is discarded when `applyPatch` throws.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Callers can treat `applyPatch` as atomic: on success they get a fully new document sharing no mutable
+  state with their inputs; on failure (invalid patch or a failed operation) they get a thrown error and
+  their original `document`/`patch` values are provably untouched.
+- A future caller wanting the URI fragment form of a pointer, or wanting partial/best-effort patch
+  application instead of all-or-nothing, must handle that outside `applyPatch`, consistent with how
+  `resolvePointer` already scopes itself to the plain pointer string form.
