@@ -0,0 +1,46 @@
+---
+id: task-002-implement-json-patch-rfc-6902-applypatch
+type: task
+title: "Implement JSON Patch RFC 6902 applyPatch"
+status: approved
+---
+
+## Request
+
+Implement JSON Patch (RFC 6902) with its six operations: `add`, `remove`, `replace`, `move`, `copy`
+and `test`. Export `applyPatch(document, patch)` from `src/index.ts`; it applies the patch (an array of
+operations) to a JSON document and returns the resulting document as a new value without mutating its
+input. It throws when the patch is invalid, or when one of its operations fails.
+
+## What was done
+
+- Wrote `src/index.test.ts` first (red): 26 new tests covering each operation's happy path (object/array
+  add, array `-` append, root-pointer add/replace, object/array remove and replace, move between object
+  members and within an array, copy with source/destination independence, test success) and error paths
+  (missing intermediate path, out-of-bounds/`-` array indices for remove/replace, missing target for
+  replace/move/copy, `move` with `from` a proper prefix of `path` or equal to the root pointer, `remove`
+  of the root pointer, failed `test`), a multi-operation sequence, input-immutability (document and the
+  patch's own `value` objects untouched after the call), and malformed-patch/operation validation
+  (non-array patch, missing `path`/`value`/`from`, unknown `op`). Confirmed all new tests failed before
+  any implementation existed (`applyPatch` did not exist).
+- Refactored `resolvePointer` in `src/index.ts` to share its pointer-parsing (`parsePointer`) and
+  strict-navigation (`walk`) logic with the new patch code, without changing its observable behaviour —
+  the existing `resolvePointer` tests still pass unmodified.
+- Implemented `applyPatch`: deep-clones the input document (`structuredClone`) into a `{ value }`
+  wrapper so the root pointer can be treated like any other container member (no special-casing for
+  `add`/`replace`/`test`/`copy` at the root), applies each operation to that private clone in sequence
+  (so a later invalid/failing operation leaves the original `document` untouched), and returns
+  `root.value`. `add`/`move`/`copy` share an `insertMember` helper that accepts `-`/in-range indices for
+  arrays; `remove`/`replace` use helpers requiring an already-existing index/member; `copy`/`move`/`add`
+  deep-clone the value being inserted so the result never aliases the patch's `value` or another part of
+  the document. Added a recursive `deepEqual` for `test`. Ran the suite again: all 48 tests pass (green).
+- Recorded the RFC-ambiguous choices (root-pointer handling per operation, `move`'s proper-prefix
+  rejection, `-` only for insertion, clone-on-insert for no aliasing, structural equality for `test`,
+  per-operation validation) as `dl-002-json-patch-rfc-6902-semantics-for-applypatch`.
+- Documented `applyPatch` with a usage example in `README.md` and a doc comment on the exported function
+  and the exported `JsonPatchOperation` type.
+
+## Verification
+
+- `npm test` — 48/48 passing.
+- `npm run typecheck` — no errors.
